---
import { getChapters, splitChapter } from '../lib/chapter-order';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const chapters = await getChapters();
---

<Layout title="Learn Me a Threat">
  <h1>Learn Me a Threat</h1>

  <aside class="callout callout-note">
    <p>
      This blog is still very much a work in progress. If this is your
      first time here and you're interested, maybe come back in a few
      weeks when there's more content.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section class="about">
    <p>
      I'm Zach. I just graduated grad school with a Master's degree in
      Physics, where I spent the last few years studying how quantum
      computers threaten Bitcoin. Along the way, I picked up a decent sense
      of how quantum computers affect other cryptocurrencies too.
    </p>
    <p>
      I didn't have some lifelong destiny pulling me toward this, I kind of
      stumbled into it. I started my Master's mostly because quantum computing
      was hyped up at the time and I was curious about it after some exposure
      to it in undergrad research. When it came time to pick a thesis topic, I
      landed on quantum attacks against Bitcoin for two reasons: it's a real,
      direct application of quantum computing that anyone can actually
      understand, not some esoteric thing only other physicists would get the
      point of. Also, crypto and NFTs were huge at the time too, like Bored
      Ape NFTs selling for millions of dollars before they became worthless,
      so that definitely played into it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I knew basically nothing about crypto or Bitcoin going in, but the more
      I read the more I got hooked on one specific thing about it. This is a
      system where you're sending money back and forth to total strangers, no
      bank in the middle, no company you can call if something goes wrong,
      nobody vouching for anybody. You'd think that means it shouldn't work
      at all, since nobody actually trusts anybody in it. But it does work,
      because the trust isn't coming from people or institutions in the first
      place, it's coming from the math underneath it. Once that clicked for
      me, I couldn't really let it go, and that fascination is basically what
      turned into my thesis, which turned into a paper that's on <a
      href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.23952">arXiv</a> now, in the process
      of getting published.
    </p>
    <p>
      Honestly, the truth is I really like yapping about this stuff, and I
      don't have an outlet for that anymore. I
      just find the topic genuinely interesting and like teaching it. Some of
      my fondest memories from grad school are from teaching, as long as it
      was on something I actually cared about. This blog is meant to bring
      that feeling back.
    </p>
    <p>
      Think of this blog as a museum of my thoughts while they're still
      fresh. If you want to follow along and find it interesting, that's a
      nice bonus. But I do think there's real value in this content existing
      on its own. How quantum computers affect cryptocurrencies and
      blockchains in general is a broader topic than people realize, and the
      information is scattered across a pile of papers, journals, and
      reports. I want to be as comprehensive as I can and put it all in one
      place. I won't cover every single attack or edge case, there's too
      many, but I want to give a real lay of the land: how each attack
      works, what's being thought about it, and what could be (or is being)
      done.
    </p>
    <p>
      You don't need any prior knowledge of Bitcoin, cryptography, or
      quantum computing. My goal is to make this accessible to anyone,
      explaining things rigorously from the ground up, in as plain language
      as I can manage.
    </p>
    <p>
      This blog, and its name, are inspired by <a
      href="https://learnmeabitcoin.com">learnmeabitcoin.com</a> by Greg
      Walker. It does a genuinely great job explaining Bitcoin's inner
      workings in plain language, accessible to basically anyone. I won't go
      nearly as deep into Bitcoin's mechanics here, that's not really the
      point. If that's what you're after, go check out Greg's page instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      Last thing, a disclaimer. I dislike writing and I'm slow at it, which
      sounds a bit paradoxical given that's the whole point of a blog. But
      I like this stuff more than I dislike writing about it, so writing is
      just the tax I pay to get this stuff out of my head and into something
      readable. I use AI
      (mainly Claude) to help with that part. My process is: write out my
      raw thoughts and ideas, then use AI to help with structure, clarity,
      and flow, verifying accuracy along the way. The ideas, rigor, and
      explanations are mine, but to be precise about it, parts of this blog
      are AI-assisted. If that's a dealbreaker, no hard feelings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 class="contents-heading">Contents</h2>

  {chapters.size === 0 ? (
    <div class="empty-state">
      <h2>No posts yet.</h2>
      <p>Check back soon &mdash; the first post is on its way.</p>
    </div>
  ) : (
    [...chapters].map(([chapterName, chapterPosts]) => {
      const { sectionPost, subsectionPosts } = splitChapter(chapterPosts);
      return (
        <section class="chapter">
          <h2 class="chapter-label">{chapterName}</h2>
          {sectionPost ? (
            <>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li>
                  <a class="post-title" href={`/posts/${sectionPost.data.slug}/`}>{sectionPost.data.title}</a>
                </li>
              </ul>
              {subsectionPosts.length > 0 && (
                <ul class="subsection-list">
                  {subsectionPosts.map((post) => (
                    <li>
                      <a class="post-title" href={`/posts/${post.data.slug}/`}>{post.data.title}</a>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              )}
            </>
          ) : (
            <ul class="post-list">
            {chapterPosts.map((post) => (
              <li>
                <a class="post-title" href={`/posts/${post.data.slug}/`}>{post.data.title}</a>
              </li>
            ))}
            </ul>
          )}
        </section>
      );
    })
  )}
</Layout>

<style>
  .contents-heading {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .post-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .post-title {
    font-size: 24px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .chapter-label {
    font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
  }

  .chapter + .chapter {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .subsection-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
</style>
